WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.267 --> 00:00:11.316
This video shows how to put an animation from Pixodesk 2D Animator into a React app, as a Lottie file. It takes one package, one file and one component.

2
00:00:12.533 --> 00:00:14.884
Let's start with the app as it is.

3
00:00:19.033 --> 00:00:21.463
A heading, and nothing that moves yet.

4
00:00:22.700 --> 00:00:28.186
To play Lottie files, the app needs a Lottie player. Let's install lottie react.

5
00:00:33.767 --> 00:00:36.065
Lottie react is in, one package.

6
00:00:37.300 --> 00:00:43.204
Now for the animation itself. Let's switch to the editor, where a football is already drawn.

7
00:00:44.733 --> 00:00:47.476
It's a vector drawing, standing still.

8
00:00:48.700 --> 00:00:51.443
To make it move, let's switch on Auto mode.

9
00:00:53.933 --> 00:00:57.381
With Auto mode on, every change becomes a keyframe.

10
00:00:58.600 --> 00:01:02.100
Half a second in, let's drag the ball down to the floor.

11
00:01:05.233 --> 00:01:08.917
A keyframe appears on the timeline, holding the new position.

12
00:01:10.133 --> 00:01:12.197
At one second, let's bring it back up.

13
00:01:15.533 --> 00:01:19.034
Now it falls and rises again, in one second.

14
00:01:20.267 --> 00:01:26.797
A real ball speeds up as it falls, and slows down as it rises. Let's set that easing.

15
00:01:33.933 --> 00:01:38.113
Gravity easing, falling and rising, makes the bounce feel natural.

16
00:01:39.333 --> 00:01:45.577
The animation is ready. Let's save it as Lottie JSON, straight into the project.

17
00:01:50.700 --> 00:01:52.764
Now, back to our app.

18
00:01:54.300 --> 00:01:57.644
The animation file is already there, next to the code.

19
00:01:58.867 --> 00:02:02.550
Let's import the Lottie component, and the animation file.

20
00:02:11.467 --> 00:02:16.534
The component comes from lottie react, the animation from the JSON file.

21
00:02:17.767 --> 00:02:20.065
Then, let's place it on the page.

22
00:02:30.733 --> 00:02:37.368
Its source is the animation. Autoplay starts it when the page loads, and loop repeats it.

23
00:02:38.600 --> 00:02:41.082
Let's reload the page, and watch.

24
00:02:46.200 --> 00:02:50.536
There it is. The ball bounces, right inside the React app.

25
00:02:52.567 --> 00:03:01.474
So, one package, one Lottie file saved from the editor, and one component. That's all it takes to bring an animation into a React app.
